\section{Verbalized Self-Evolution}
\label{sec:verbalized}

Passive v9 (\S\ref{sec:experiments}) re-reads the normal reference images
on every test query but never \emph{accumulates} anything from them:
the same ref set produces the same ad-hoc reasoning trace a thousand
times. The retrieval-based active-learning pilot we originally
attempted (pre-review appendix, cf.\ \citet{al_pilot_appendix})
partially addressed this by injecting a DINOv2-CLS-retrieved neighbour
prior, but was bottlenecked by what visual-similarity embeddings can
express: on \emph{logical} anomalies (wrong count, misordered
components) no DINOv2-CLS neighbour encodes the defining predicate,
and the pilot indeed regressed $-4.0$ pp on D5 MVTec-LOCO.

This section introduces a \textbf{verbalized self-evolution} protocol
that learns a persistent, natural-language \emph{rulebook} per domain
(or per class, where applicable) and injects it into the agent's
turn-$1$ system prompt at test time. Because rules are natural
language, they can capture predicates the visual embedding cannot
(``count the legs'', ``screws should be aligned''), and because the
rulebook is produced \emph{once} per domain, its cost amortises across
all test queries in that domain.

\subsection{Three learning regimes}
\label{ssec:verb_regimes}

We frame this as a ladder of three learning regimes; the choice is
about \emph{how much supervision the system touches}, not whether a
rulebook is used at inference time:

\begin{description}
\item[\textbf{Passive v9}] — training-free. No rulebook; refs are read
  per-query as in \S\ref{ssec:main_results}. This is the baseline.
\item[\textbf{L1 — ref-only rulebook} (self-supervised, $0$ oracle)]
  — reflect on the normal reference pool and emit a persistent
  rulebook containing a \emph{normal signature} and a set of
  \emph{hypothesized anomaly modes}. Uses zero oracle labels. The
  rulebook \emph{is} a learned artifact; we therefore do not call L1
  training-free, despite it touching no new labels.
\item[\textbf{L2 — active verbalized rulebook} (semi-supervised,
  $K{=}10$ oracle per domain)] — run passive v9 on dev, pick the $K$
  highest-uncertainty dev items ($|s{-}0.5|$ smallest), obtain their
  ground-truth labels from the dev manifest, and reflect each
  (image, agent trajectory, label) tuple into a \emph{corrective
  rule}. Only dev items are revealed; test labels are never touched.
\item[\textbf{L1+L2 — stacked rulebook}] — concatenate the L1 rulebook
  and the L2 corrective rules into a single domain rulebook.
  Oracle budget is $K$ per domain (same as L2 alone).
\end{description}

L1 and L2 capture complementary biases. L1 reads only normal images
and therefore has a \emph{predict-normal bias}: its
\emph{hypothesized anomaly modes} are reasoned from the function and
structure of the ref objects, but never from a real anomaly, so the
rulebook tends to over-accept borderline queries. L2 reads exactly
the dev items Passive v9 is most uncertain about, so its corrective
rules are concentrated on borderline cases and naturally pull in the
opposite direction — adding anomaly modes for dev false-negatives and
tolerances for dev false-positives. The L1+L2 stack therefore tests
whether the two sources combine super-additively.

\subsection{Method}
\label{ssec:verb_method}

\paragraph{Rulebook granularity.} We use an adaptive granularity that
follows the dataset schema rather than a tuned hyperparameter: for
domains with more than one class in the \texttt{manifests\_v2}
taxonomy (D1 industrial / $15$ classes, D2 retail / $6$, D3
VisA / $12$, D4 infrastructure / $3$, D5 MVTec-LOCO / $5$, D6
Real3D-AD / $11$) we build a \emph{per-class} rulebook; for the
six single-class domains (D7--D12) we build a single
\emph{per-domain} rulebook. At test time the rulebook for the query's
(domain, class) tuple is injected; the lookup uses the manifest
\texttt{category} field, not an oracle class prediction from the
model.

\paragraph{L1 protocol.} For each rulebook unit (class or single-class
domain), sample $\min(8,\;|\mathrm{refs}|)$ reference images. A
reflector VLM — the \emph{same} backbone used for the query-time
agent, no stronger model — is asked to emit a strict-JSON rulebook of
the form
\begin{quote}\small\ttfamily
\{"normal\_signature":["...","..."],
  "hypothesized\_anomaly\_modes":["...","..."],\\
 "rules":["if X then lean anomaly","if Y then lean normal"]\}
\end{quote}
The two-field decomposition is a hard requirement of the prompt:
requiring \emph{both} a normal signature and an enumerated anomaly
mode list is our primary counter-measure against L1's predict-normal
bias, and we measure its effect directly
(\S\ref{ssec:verb_bias_diagnostic}).
The reflector sees only ref images, no query and no oracle — so L1
uses zero dev or test exposure.

\paragraph{L2 protocol.} Run Passive v9 on all $40$ dev items per
domain. Let $\mathcal{U}_K$ be the set of $K{=}10$ dev items with the
smallest $|s{-}0.5|$. For each $i\in\mathcal{U}_K$, reveal its
manifest label $y_i$. A reflector VLM receives
$(x_i,\;\text{agent trajectory}_i,\;y_i,\;\text{L1 rulebook})$ and emits
either (a) an \emph{anomaly-mode extension} when $y_i{=}1$ and the
agent missed it, or (b) a \emph{normal-signature tolerance} when
$y_i{=}0$ and the agent over-flagged. The reflector writes at most
one rule per dev item. The remaining $30$ dev items are held out as
a \textbf{rule-quality holdout} (\S\ref{ssec:verb_holdout}) and are
not touched by the reflector.

\paragraph{Stacking.} The L1+L2 rulebook is the string concatenation
\texttt{[L1 signature] $\parallel$ [L1 anomaly modes $\cup$ L2
extensions] $\parallel$ [L1 rules $\cup$ L2 corrective rules]}, with
de-duplication at the rule-string level. Rules coming from L2 are
marked ``(corrective)''; we leave a principled conflict resolution
between L1 and L2 predicates to future work.

\paragraph{Test-time injection.} The rulebook for (domain, class) is
prepended to the turn-$1$ user message of the Passive v9 agent as a
short JSON block tagged \texttt{DOMAIN RULEBOOK}. All other
machinery — mode classifier, refutation protocol, tool set, stopping
criterion — is unchanged. No test labels or test-set items enter the
rulebook pipeline at any stage; the injection is the only test-time
difference from Passive v9.

\subsection{Experiments}
\label{ssec:verb_exp}

\paragraph{Setup.} All 12 CrossDomainVAD-11 test domains
($n{=}1{,}418$), Qwen3.5-VL-27B backbone, temperature $0$. Each
variant is run with $3$ seeds; we report the mean across seeds.
Oracle budget for L2 is $K{=}10$ dev items per domain ($120$ total
across the benchmark). All hyperparameters are frozen across domains.
Reflector calls are charged explicitly in Table~\ref{tab:verb_cost}.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\caption{\textbf{Verbalized self-evolution: per-domain AUROC,
Qwen3.5-VL-27B test.} Each cell is the mean of $3$ seeds; oracle
column shows cumulative revealed dev labels. Adaptive granularity:
per-class for D1/D2/D3/D4/D5/D6, per-domain for D7/D8/D9/D10/D11/D12.
All test items are held strictly out of the rulebook-construction
pipeline.}
\label{tab:verb_main}
\small
\setlength{\tabcolsep}{4pt}
\begin{tabular}{lccccc}
\toprule
Variant & Oracle / domain & Rulebook source & Macro AUROC & $\Delta$ vs Passive & $P(\Delta{>}0)$ \\
\midrule
Passive v9 (\S\ref{ssec:main_results}) & $0$ & --- & $0.814$ & --- & --- \\
\textbf{+ L1}   (ref-only, self-sup)     & $0$   & refs only           & \emph{t.b.d.} & \emph{t.b.d.} & \emph{t.b.d.} \\
\textbf{+ L2}   (active verbalized)      & $10$  & dev oracle only     & \emph{t.b.d.} & \emph{t.b.d.} & \emph{t.b.d.} \\
\textbf{+ L1+L2} (stacked)               & $10$  & refs + dev oracle   & \emph{t.b.d.} & \emph{t.b.d.} & \emph{t.b.d.} \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\paragraph{Bias diagnostic.}
\label{ssec:verb_bias_diagnostic}
For each variant we report the \emph{predict-normal rate} on the
test set (fraction of queries with anomaly score $<0.5$). Our working
hypothesis is that L1-alone will show a higher predict-normal rate
than Passive, L2-alone will show a lower rate, and the L1+L2 stack
will land closest to Passive.

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\small
\begin{tabular}{lcc}
\toprule
Variant & Predict-normal rate (test) & $\Delta$ vs Passive \\
\midrule
Passive v9 & \emph{t.b.d.} & $0$ \\
+ L1 & \emph{t.b.d.} & \emph{t.b.d.} \\
+ L2 & \emph{t.b.d.} & \emph{t.b.d.} \\
+ L1+L2 & \emph{t.b.d.} & \emph{t.b.d.} \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\caption{Bias diagnostic; a signed $\Delta$ measures the predict-normal
drift induced by the rulebook.}
\label{tab:verb_bias}
\end{table}

\paragraph{Rule-quality holdout.}
\label{ssec:verb_holdout}
The $30$ dev items that the L2 reflector did not see form a held-out
validation set for rulebook quality. We report dev-holdout AUROC of
each variant; this measures rulebook quality without touching the
test split and disentangles ``the rulebook is better'' from ``the
test set happens to benefit.''

\paragraph{Qualitative rule samples.} For each domain we include
one representative rule from the L1 and L2 rulebooks
(Appendix~\ref{app:verbalized_rules}), with its associated test-item
flip. Example rule classes we expect to see: counting / logical (D5),
symmetry / bilateral (D9 brain MRI), texture anomaly (D7, D8),
scene-level anomaly (D12), missing-component (D1).

\paragraph{Ablations.}
We report three ablations in Appendix~\ref{app:verbalized_ablations}:
(a) removing the \texttt{hypothesized\_anomaly\_modes} field from the
L1 prompt (tests whether the bias counter-measure works),
(b) per-class vs pooled per-domain rulebook on the multi-class
domains (tests whether class-level granularity is needed),
(c) replacing the dev-oracle in L2 with the same-budget random dev
labels (tests whether uncertainty selection matters).

\subsection{Limitations}
\label{ssec:verb_limitations}
L1 reads only normal refs and therefore cannot learn truly novel
anomaly classes unless the reflector hallucinates them; L2 is bounded
by the $K$ oracle budget and by the dev set's coverage of the
anomaly manifold; the stacked L1+L2 rulebook does not yet resolve
predicate conflicts between L1 and L2 formally and can in principle
loop if L1 and L2 recommend opposite actions on the same feature.
The rulebook is currently static across test items within a
(domain, class); a dynamic variant that selects rules per-query is
future work.

=== END §5 ===

The t.b.d. in tables are placeholders — experiments running in background. Do not penalize them.

