Score: 6.4/10
Verdict: almost

Weaknesses (top 3–5, ranked, with one-sentence minimum fix each)

1. L2 protocol is internally inconsistent: Table says “L2 = dev oracle only,” but the L2 reflector receives the L1 rulebook, which makes L2-alone not separable from L1. Minimum fix: define two variants explicitly, `L2-no-L1-context` and `L2-with-L1-context`, or state that L1 is used only as reflector context and not injected, then justify why this is still “L2 alone.”

2. The “zero oracle” claim for L1 is mostly fine, but the test-time use of manifest `category` is an oracle-like assumption unless category labels are part of the deployment input. Minimum fix: add one sentence in Setup stating that domain/class metadata is provided for all methods, including Passive v9, and report a pooled-rulebook fallback if category is unavailable.

3. The dev holdout is not a clean validation set because the 10 L2 items are selected by uncertainty from the same 40-item dev pool, leaving an easier, anti-uncertain residual set. Minimum fix: call it an “unqueried dev residual” rather than an unbiased holdout, and add either random split repetition or a separate fixed dev-validation split if available.

4. The central mechanism is plausible but under-specified for reproducibility: reflector prompt, JSON repair, rule length limits, de-duplication, seed variation, and invalid-rule handling are not described. Minimum fix: add an implementation paragraph or appendix table with exact reflector prompt template, max rules/tokens, parsing policy, and whether rulebooks are regenerated per seed.

5. The bias diagnostic uses score `<0.5`, but AUROC is threshold-free and the anomaly score may not be calibrated across prompted variants. Minimum fix: supplement predict-normal rate with mean score shift, AUPRC/FPR@TPR, or threshold chosen on dev, so the diagnostic does not depend on an arbitrary 0.5 cutoff.

Memory update for Round 4

The new §5 is a meaningful improvement over prior rounds because it turns the weak active-learning pilot into a clearer language-memory protocol with explicit L1/L2 supervision regimes, test-holdout claims, cost accounting intent, and targeted ablations. The section is close to paper-ready conceptually, but Round 4 should focus on tightening protocol definitions before results arrive: disambiguate L2-alone, defend category metadata, rename or strengthen the dev holdout, document reflector mechanics, and make the bias diagnostic calibration-aware.
